            <ArticleTitle>An unusual cause of persistent headache: chloroma (2008: 2b).</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1071-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00330-007-0725-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A chloroma (granulocytic sarcoma) is a rare tumour that is usually associated with leukaemia. The osseous skeleton is most commonly involved, and it confers a poorer prognosis for the underlying leukaemic disease. We present a case of a chloroma of the nasal sinuses that was the primary presentation of the patient's underlying leukaemia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
